due to the way mocha handles `unhandledRejections` and `uncaughtExceptions` this can create situations in which the errors don't immediately indicate what happened.

Because the `on(message, cb)` handler is called asynchronous - if you were to throw in here it would not be caught by the promise - it'll become a global uncaught exception event.

This can be mitigated by a number of ways like this...

```js
return new Promise((resolve) => {
  Io.client(`http://localhost:${HTTP_PORT}`, {
    agent: this.agent,
    transports: ['websocket']
  })
  .on('message', resolve)
})
.then((msg) => {
  expect(msg).to.eq('It worked!')
  
  if (this.debugProxy) {
    expect(this.debugProxy.requests[0].ws).to.be.true
    expect(this.debugProxy.requests[0].url).to.include('http://localhost:31080')
  }
})
```

Doing it this way will ensure that `msg` always resolves the promise which then allows you to carry that value forward and the promise chain is always hooked up correctly. I don't think we do this very often in other tests though - so you won't find very many good examples.

